 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "K_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Aaron Sorkin's 'The Social Reckoning' (Sony), about Facebook whistleblower Frances Haugen and the Facebook Files, opened in American theaters on 9 October 2026 to about $5.5 million, well below the $7-8 million forecast, and was widely called a flop/bomb. Narration: 'The Social Reckoning bombed. The truth? Worse.' (the second sentence is editorial framing for what follows). Headline 'THE SOCIAL RECKONING FLOP / THE TRUTH IS WORSE'.",
   "sources": [
    "ap_abc4",
    "ap_wdiv",
    "wrap",
    "avclub",
    "slash",
    "variety"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_tease",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Tease of item 3 (the figure is attributed in s04 and on screen): Meta's internal documents, as reported by Reuters, projected about $16 billion of 2024 revenue from ads for scams and banned goods. Narration: 'Make it $16 billion worse.' Label 'META'S OWN PROJECTION, PER REUTERS'.",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "mediapost",
    "san",
    "medianama"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_flop",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Opening weekend (9-11 October 2026): about $5.5 million from 2,606 North American theaters, fifth place (Sunday studio estimate; one outlet reported $5.3 million). Narration: 'It opened with $5.5 million.' Bar label '2026: $5.5M (studio estimate)'.",
   "sources": [
    "ap_abc4",
    "ap_wdiv",
    "wrap",
    "avclub",
    "slash"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_original",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The Social Network opened to $22.4 million in October 2010 (initial estimate $23 million), about four times The Social Reckoning's opening. Narration: 'The original? Four times that.' Bar label '2010: $22.4M'.",
   "sources": [
    "numbers",
    "hdroom",
    "tc10",
    "wrap"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_haugen",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "In 2021 Frances Haugen, a former Facebook product manager, leaked thousands of internal documents (reported by the Wall Street Journal as 'The Facebook Files' from September 2021) and testified to a Senate subcommittee (5 October 2021). Narration: 'In 2021, Frances Haugen leaked the Facebook Files.' then 'That part got the movie.' (the film dramatises Haugen and the Facebook Files; editorial bridge to item 3)",
   "sources": [
    "aj21",
    "npr21",
    "aa21",
    "wh21",
    "variety"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_slide",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "WORDING AS REPORTED (card): a 2019 internal Facebook research slide said 'We make body image issues worse for one in three teen girls' (WSJ, as reported by Al Jazeera and others); Facebook said its research was mischaracterised. Card 'WE MAKE BODY IMAGE ISSUES WORSE FOR ONE IN THREE TEEN GIRLS' / '2019 internal slide, via the WSJ (2021)'.",
   "sources": [
    "aj21",
    "wh21",
    "npr21"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_reuters",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "A Reuters investigation (November 2025, Jeff Horwitz) was based on internal Meta documents. Narration: 'But Reuters reported on Meta's own documents.'",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "mediapost",
    "san",
    "medianama"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_16bn",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "STATISTIC (counter): Meta's internal documents, as reported by Reuters, projected that about 10 % of its 2024 revenue, about $16 billion, would come from ads for scams and banned goods. Narration: 'They projected $16 billion from ads for scams and banned goods.' Counter '$16,000,000,000 / PROJECTED, SCAM + BANNED-GOODS ADS'.",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "mediapost",
    "san",
    "medianama",
    "blumenthal",
    "bnn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_denial",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "META'S RESPONSE (on screen in s05 and s06): spokesman Andy Stone said the documents present a selective view that distorts Meta's approach, called the 10.1 % estimate 'rough and overly-inclusive' (Meta later found the figure was lower; no updated number given), and said Meta fights fraud aggressively. Label 'META: ESTIMATE WAS \"ROUGH AND OVERLY-INCLUSIVE\"'.",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "bandt",
    "san",
    "bnn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_95",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Per the documents as reported by Reuters, Meta banned advertisers only when its automated systems predicted at least 95 % certainty that they were committing fraud. Narration: 'Banning took 95 percent certainty of fraud.'",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "mediapost",
    "bnn",
    "indaily"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_rates",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN-FACT-STYLE LIKE MOMENT: if Meta was less certain but still believed an advertiser was a likely scammer, it charged higher ad rates as a penalty, according to the documents (to dissuade them). Narration: 'Below that? Higher ad rates.' Meter tag 'AD PRICE ↑'.",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "mediapost"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_daily",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "A December 2024 document estimated Meta's platforms showed users about 15 billion 'higher-risk' scam ads a day (as reported by Reuters). Label in s06 '15 BILLION \"HIGHER-RISK\" SCAM ADS A DAY'.",
   "sources": [
    "khaleej",
    "dstar",
    "medianama",
    "reuters_pz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_tip",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "SAVE TIP (overlay, official, for the viewer): the FTC says social media ads are not always vetted, prices much, much lower than elsewhere are probably a scam, and scammers ask for gift cards, wire transfers, payment apps or cryptocurrency; Action Fraud warns of investment ads using fake celebrity endorsements. Rows 'WAY TOO CHEAP? LIKELY SCAM', 'CELEB \"INVESTMENT\" AD? NO', 'GIFT CARD OR CRYPTO? NO'.",
   "sources": [
    "ftc_ads",
    "ftc_low",
    "ftc_top",
    "af_crypto",
    "af_celeb"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_horwitz",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN FACT: Jeff Horwitz, the Wall Street Journal reporter behind the Facebook Files, is portrayed in The Social Reckoning (by Jeremy Allen White; not shown); after joining Reuters in 2025 he reported the scam-ads investigation (the film does not draw on that later reporting). Narration: 'The film's reporter, Jeff Horwitz, broke this story.'",
   "sources": [
    "opc",
    "pulitzer",
    "reuters_pz",
    "khaleej",
    "variety"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_pulitzer",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Reuters (Jeff Horwitz with Engen Tham) won the 2026 Pulitzer Prize for Beat Reporting (announced 4 May 2026) for its investigations of Meta, including the fraudulent-ad revenue. Card row 'AND WON A 2026 PULITZER'.",
   "sources": [
    "pulitzer",
    "reuters_pz",
    "gma"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_endfilm",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Callback: the film's opening weekend, about $5.5 million (studio estimate). Narration (s07, second line): 'The film opened at $5.5 million.'",
   "sources": [
    "ap_abc4",
    "ap_wdiv",
    "wrap",
    "avclub",
    "slash"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "K_end16",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Punchline: Meta's own documents, as reported by Reuters, projected about $16 billion from ads for scams and banned goods (Meta disputes the estimate). Narration (last line): 'Scam ads paid a projected $16 billion.' (the ads' projected revenue to Meta) Status label: senators asked the FTC and SEC to investigate (Nov 2025); lawsuits by the U.S. Virgin Islands, the Consumer Federation of America and Santa Clara County are pending allegations.",
